#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115105 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115282 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "4f9e91fd-9896-5fdd-8a60-b8ec86ca761c" :knowledge-point/title "Graphing a Translated or Stretched Logarithmic Function"}] :question/id #uuid "4228cae9-1914-59e9-8ed6-48b3edf92638" :question/math-academy-id "e-5734" :question/problem "Plot the curve of $y=\\log_7 (x+4).$" :question/worked-solution "To plot $y=\\log_7 {(x+4)},$ we apply the following steps:\n\n- Take the curve $y=\\log_7 {x}.$\n\n![](images/97/975f58b320e2293dbfaf267a3acfe9187438ae40add3603bac98d142b35c44c1.png)\n\n- Translate it by $4$ units to the left to get the graph of $y=\\log_7 {(x+4)}.$\n\n![](images/ce/ce5db8eb1adb7e84337427c1c769eabec9cb420b51a7e867f47e8d573c697a79.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115106 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115283 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "f44d9ddf-a830-559a-807e-a5a3eaaf66e8" :knowledge-point/title "Finding Unknown Parameters Given a Transformed Logarithmic Function"}] :question/id #uuid "7d064137-5763-57d8-9b8e-ea145c323427" :question/math-academy-id "e-5736" :question/problem "The diagram below shows the graph of $y=k \\log_5 (bx).$ Find the value of $b\\cdot k.$\n\n![](images/36/3698fc49edb443517ed0ee2cfa81c586c428f402df7028a8ec4b47702d8a4fad.png)" :question/worked-solution "Clearly, $b \\neq 0.$ Substituting $x=\\dfrac1b$ into the equation of the curve, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =k{log}_{5}⁡(b\\cdot \\frac{1}{b}) \\\\  & =k{log}_{5}⁡1 \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the point $\\left(\\dfrac1b, 0\\right)$ lies on the curve. But from the diagram, we know that $(2,0)$ lies on the curve. Hence, \n\n$$\n(2,0)  = \\left(\\dfrac1b,0\\right)\\quad\\Rightarrow\\quad b=\\dfrac12.\n$$\n\nNow, we substitute coordinates of the point $(10,3)$ into the equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}k{log}_{5}⁡(bx) & =y \\\\ k{log}_{5}⁡(\\frac{1}{2}x) & =y \\\\ k{log}_{5}⁡(\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 10) & =3 \\\\ k{log}_{5}⁡5 & =3 \\\\ k & =3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally,\n\n$$\nb\\cdot k = \\dfrac12 \\cdot 3 = \\dfrac32.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115147 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115324 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "d99f87fa-f746-5d01-8674-77c21bd8f309" :knowledge-point/title "Combining Translations and Stretches of Logarithmic Functions"}] :question/id #uuid "bd143c19-b7b4-5e50-9e22-10f8c505c047" :question/math-academy-id "e-8663" :question/problem "Plot the graph of ![](images/54/54999da9704e7c5f8b341e30bb7af0d8c5c89b4cd91fa8be96d71521c30a6b95.svg)" :question/worked-solution "To plot ![](images/ea/ea6a0209e0fa8abfa9adbd2a10c238e598d2df19b3039eed6f9fef358c03f0b0.svg) we apply the following steps:\n\n- Take the curve ![](images/4b/4bd2e8b39608d2ffef63de1d65e9fab3781acd8b8ea582a3a4d8357af49b1a77.svg)\n\n![](images/45/452610c472df847bb7a09815444c3d4f318008a8881864f668f153bee6cbae33.png)\n\n- Translate it by ![](images/f5/f5f3224583db57b9cfe323c8489ef5e5271000002b5ea253eb98fd067a858b2d.svg) units to the right to get the graph of ![](images/c0/c0b21b4821b92baca78e191dad50f9f3a63cf0ddd8425c966ef5a2375a78262d.svg)\n\n![](images/5b/5bf8eba8adaa2e7221d77eca13bef8ee33a37ed05af2071e12fec12f4edb672d.png)\n\n- Then, stretch the result by a scale factor of ![](images/40/402ff5d0c0c3aaa47f18c89f6d96c95d47840eac13822d596a51bdb0c5f9a4ab.svg) parallel to the ![](images/1d/1d9b846cface9f9348e4081a0dd87dd254bf26663c12aa0d1a15aa24d2393261.svg)-axis to get the graph of ![](images/ce/ce51c3344030b46eb0a3b4ed74f85e6284e4357a375c338035993147ee92f9fa.svg)\n\n![](images/90/90bd455c3a6b833b169c3207c58cc422c3fb963c7de9ae5f11bc0b6464117d6f.png)\n\n- Finally, translate the result by ![](images/95/95836ae470c61ab15bfb67d6f0c92d2ad8d03d400b04473e81e58de3ee44bf32.svg) unit up to get the graph of ![](images/54/54999da9704e7c5f8b341e30bb7af0d8c5c89b4cd91fa8be96d71521c30a6b95.svg)\n\n![](images/ad/ade4b0bff8907d7284cedd15895e997013121f0f91f2b82a0314f363a81ad3a9.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115148 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115325 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "54834980-1e19-58eb-b15f-e46e2a1aeffe" :knowledge-point/title "Finding Unknown Parameters Given a Stretched Logarithmic Function"}] :question/id #uuid "031f2ffe-6dd5-5b4f-93c0-3c1a6f1edf23" :question/math-academy-id "e-8664" :question/problem "![](images/bd/bd74c5b1a7233be5978a128a74956fa953cd78a737b8f40f68c1e42993f58140.png)\n\nGiven the diagram above, what is the value of $k?$" :question/worked-solution "The graph of $y=k\\log_2 {x}$ can be obtained from $y=\\log_2 {x}$ by a vertical stretch of scale factor $k.$ \n\nFrom the diagram, we deduce that under such a transformation, the point $(4,2)$ that lies on $y=\\log_2 {x}$ becomes\n\n$$\n(4,k \\cdot 2)=\\left(4,3\\right)\n$$\n\nwhich lies on $y=k\\log_2 {x}.$ \n\nTherefore, $k=\\dfrac 32.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186563657 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115324 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "d99f87fa-f746-5d01-8674-77c21bd8f309" :knowledge-point/title "Combining Translations and Stretches of Logarithmic Functions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3854b07d-32e7-5537-be69-cc8b3ce59bd8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/48/48418c05467ca954bb2620ed356d1db9ce3c7774c5e41c6fa8b1f20cebc17bed.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186563072} {:answer/id #uuid "4c121d05-33df-51a8-be1c-77a4c15bf74a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/a7/a7a1802a657832efcd1fd2ca8d2c34b9226b1b5324636f9779bda68b4f8646d1.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186563114} {:answer/id #uuid "5246ac67-dbfb-5501-8b44-ffe95ae64c4a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/a6/a6451cc35ed4d2a5b8241793e5ffeb165858f3e97c45461cf3259a61035ebabc.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186563131} {:answer/id #uuid "d704c739-42c2-55ac-ad13-b0d9b96eaa