 "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186562977} {:answer/id #uuid "3035fbac-63b9-5e92-a7af-7fae4221c58d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186563054} {:answer/id #uuid "3fb69761-90da-5ef3-9336-c74c1cd1883c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186563091} {:answer/id #uuid "d728cb75-8aff-5441-a02b-d2123ed28dfa" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186563416} {:answer/id #uuid "e7761c70-8716-56c9-a2cb-a35290fdff3f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{4}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186563459}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d728cb75-8aff-5441-a02b-d2123ed28dfa" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186563416} :answer-field/id #uuid "d0661678-c752-58dd-ae45-19a8a3ab5f43" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186563626}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "7ee0988d-03dd-5330-89a0-142478096881" :question/math-academy-id "q-33465" :question/problem "![](images/0c/0c09cf7f047cb341d3e2e2337ac41847d9cf5ff9435d075414a7db092aec3be2.png)\n\nThe diagram above shows the graph of $y={log}_{4}⁡(x-d)+c.$ Find the value of $\\frac{d}{c}.$" :question/worked-solution "In the given graph, the vertical asymptote is $x=4.$ So, the argument of the logarithm function should evaluate to zero at $x=4.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n4-d=0\\,⇒\\,d=4.\n$$\n\nNow, we substitute coordinates of the point $(8,3)$ into the equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{log}_{4}⁡(x-d)+c & =y \\\\ {log}_{4}⁡(x-4)+c & =y \\\\ {log}_{4}⁡(8-4)+c & =3 \\\\ {log}_{4}⁡(4)+c & =3 \\\\ 1+c & =3 \\\\ c & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally,\n\n$$\n\\frac{d}{c}=\\frac{4}{2}=2.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186563696 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115325 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "54834980-1e19-58eb-b15f-e46e2a1aeffe" :knowledge-point/title "Finding Unknown Parameters Given a Stretched Logarithmic Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "816e73c6-67e3-54cb-841a-bd3150e1f747" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186563230} {:answer/id #uuid "8b399da8-7125-56d3-91c5-c76971e45597" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186563258} {:answer/id #uuid "92c4cf5a-5e3f-5c1f-a1c4-be34f16977fb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186563273} {:answer/id #uuid "a25796f8-3bad-5125-8704-1ef508d19cdf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186563312} {:answer/id #uuid "a2bc685b-8218-5d63-afa0-3e0398dc7fe7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186563313}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "92c4cf5a-5e3f-5c1f-a1c4-be34f16977fb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186563273} :answer-field/id #uuid "98ef2340-2a64-5e76-8501-961e9be00321" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186563600}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "67b6ab53-2529-5d71-90b1-cd110fcf91e4" :question/math-academy-id "q-7869" :question/problem "![](images/56/569d6855e7ebdbd7c2e040d7f0200e72e0df71f88991085e3224570587c33767.png)\n\nThe point $A$ with coordinates $(2,\\operatorname{ln}⁡2)$ lies on the curve $y=\\operatorname{ln}⁡x,$ and the point ${A}^{′}$ with coordinates $(6,\\operatorname{ln}⁡2)$ lies on the curve $y=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(ax),$ shown above. What is the value of $a?$" :question/worked-solution "The graph of $y=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(ax)$ can be obtained from $y=\\operatorname{ln}⁡x$ by a horizontal stretch of scale factor $\\frac{1}{a}.$ \n\nFrom the diagram, we deduce that under such a transformation, the point $(2,\\operatorname{ln}⁡2)$ that lies on $y=\\operatorname{ln}⁡x$ becomes \n\n$$\n(\\frac{1}{a}\\cdot 2,\\operatorname{ln}⁡2)=(6,\\operatorname{ln}⁡2)\n$$\n\nwhich lies on $y=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(ax).$ \n\nTherefore, $a=\\frac{1}{3}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186563672 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115325 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "54834980-1e19-58eb-b15f-e46e2a1aeffe" :knowledge-point/title "Finding Unknown Parameters Given a Stretched Logarithmic Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3aabb100-1696-5ca1-860c-17c2b46c3d08" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186563077} {:answer/id #uuid "6094db19-089e-5350-8ea3-f89bfab0e6c2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186563169} {:answer/id #uuid "6167675c-e9af-5f3b-8b02-9d41b7ffb5ad" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186563171} {:answer/id #uuid "b50612f6-c9dc-55aa-a261-7f8631d54606" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{ln}⁡4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186563344} {:answer/id #uuid "ec347080-5383-5630-bfde-2eff2087a0d3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186563474}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ec347080-5383-5630-bfde-2eff2087a0d3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186563474} :answer-field/id #uuid "26a0aa5e-477f-56a9-b2e2-1c62979c6a31" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186563544}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "3efa74bc-65a6-5cab-855c-4b517911be86" :question/math-academy-id "q-7870" :question/problem "![](images/98/98b9df96585a5e7067f229ed85a2e60309fbc56865d9bdf61d98aed68ac70ac2.png)\n\nThe point $A$ with coordinates $(4,\\operatorname{ln}⁡4)$ lies on the curve $y=\\operatorname{ln}⁡x,$ and the point ${A}^{′}$ with coordinates $(1,\\operatorname{ln}⁡4)$ lies on the curve $y=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(ax),$ shown above. What is the value of $a?$" :question/worked-solution "The graph of $y=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(ax)$ can be obtained from $y=\\operatorname{ln}⁡x$ by a horizontal stretch of scale factor $\\frac{1}{a}.$ \n\nFrom the diagram, we deduce that under such a transformation, the point $(4,\\operatorname{ln}⁡4)$ that lies on $y=\\operatorname{ln}⁡x$ becomes \n\n$$\n(\\frac{1}{a}\\cdot 4,\\operatorname{ln}⁡4)=(1,\\operatorname{ln}⁡4)\n$$\n\nwhich lies on $y=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(ax).$ \n\nTherefore, $a=4.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186563674 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115324 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "d99f87fa-f746-5d01-8674-77c21bd8f309" :knowledge-point/title "Combining Translations and Stretches of Logarithmic Functions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "11c9eccc-a983-5339-8a4d-2d4e3ca8e1df" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/81/81645f67d82542d23b38597a7bbb682028e8e59a8449f6c9d0beac96621d081b.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186562980} {:answer/id #uuid "152301c1-86ca-5115-ba98-91bad9da541b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/9d/9dcd915da2122631cf6d7555668cd69ed15265e113acc99d9ad062ceee0090be.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186562990} {:answer/id #uuid "541193ee-865c-528d-a776-7703a6b15d27" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/6a/6a94e8b714eb5d548a271e4ba5288c355b7b77a2e54d46795a2fe5c952741649.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186563134} {:answer/id #uuid "655db017-19aa-5b27-bf3c-294f8cda967e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/45/45ad8d9d4e4ebf6b0d581ae4415dbbdad923622383d7b56278334a5eb151c0fe.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186563178} {:answer/id #uuid "7bdcccdb-e57d-5f04-a7e6-7654ae8f2721" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/94/94ae95babe9a30030c18d67347b59999e3e25c201afe8aebf5ae4197c42138d6.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186563217}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7bdcccdb-e57d-5f04-a7e6-7654ae8f2721" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/94/94ae95babe9a30030c18d67347b59999e3e25c201afe8aebf5ae4197c42138d6.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186563217} :answer-field/id #uuid "a264fcee-13bb-5baf-b833-1dc8f72ed71a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186563607}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "416b8a87-9deb-532d-86eb-39a4fa9daecb" :question/math-academy-id "q-98092" :question/problem "Which of the following is the graph of $y=2\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+3)?$" :question/worked-solution "To plot $y=2\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+3),$ we apply the following steps:\n\n- Take the curve $y=\\operatorname{ln}⁡x.$\n\n![](images/f1/f12e7576bb07678189e775c8e2cc98f621e740b45c558c743423a707253c9f81.png)\n\n- Translate it by $3$ units to the left to get the graph of $y=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+3).$\n\n![](images/e3/e313515347d6bebbaa76d047fd0900a5e712f64bed05c796907a54bf81628201.png)\n\n- Then, stretch the result by a scale factor of $2$ parallel to the $y$-axis to get the graph of $y=2\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+3).$\n\n![](images/94/94ae95babe9a30030c18d67347b59999e3e25c201afe8aebf5ae4197c42138d6.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186563703 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115283 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "f44d9ddf-a830-559a-807e-a5a3eaaf66e8" :knowledge-point/title "Finding Unknown Parameters Given a Transformed Logarithmic Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "75c99027-a990-59e8-84c8-d409c6f5f2c8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186563205} {:answer/id #uuid "8a753746-656b-5851-961e-7895d0368ce1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186563252} {:answer/id #uuid "ab9acb21-3d7e-5418-97ba-19ff056536c8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186563332} {:answer/id #uuid "ac31a6ee-2e8d-52cd-b56a-12f4039e5b52" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186563334} {:answer/id #uuid "c139792e-bada-5b41-886d-9250900c5b59" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186563370}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c139792e-bada-5b41-886d-9250900c5b59" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186563370} :answer-field/id #uuid "27ae073a-5203-5077-b949-ae499f36c0ca" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186563545}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "736b21cf-cbb6-5fc6-8626-c4e49709882d" :question/math-academy-id "q-98150" :question/problem "![](images/65/6583ad253785716c7ead3bb45083a6498fdf45b48c4572a7339b2e8c6f43266a.png)\n\nThe diagram above shows the graph of $y=k{log}_{2}⁡(bx).$ Find the value of $k\\cdot b.$" :question/worked-solution "Clearly, $b\\ne 0.$ Substituting $x=\\frac{1}{b}$ into the equation of the curve, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =k{log}_{2}⁡(b\\cdot \\frac{1}{b}) \\\\  & =k{log}_{2}⁡1 \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the point $(\\frac{1}{b},0)$ lies on the curve. But from the diagram, we know that $(3,0)$ lies on the curve. Hence\n\n$$\n(3,0)=(\\frac{1}{b},0)\\,⇒\\,b=\\frac{1}{3}.\n$$\n\nNow, we substitute coordinates of the point $(6,2)$ into the equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}k{log}_{2}⁡(bx) & =y \\\\ k{log}_{2}⁡(\\frac{1}{3}x) & =y \\\\ k{log}_{2}⁡(\\frac{1}{3}\\cdot 6) & =2 \\\\ k{log}_{2}⁡2 & =2 \\\\ k & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, \n\n$$\nk\\cdot b=2\\cdot \\frac{1}{3}=\\frac{2}{3}.\n$$"}]}
